[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says: one issue, one fix queued. Its reports say something else.
Every report in it is about a booking on the wrong day, so it was merged by
[subject](/kb/triage/dedupe). Read by what the date did, the reports fall into two problems:
a day and month that swap places, and a date that moves by one day. The fix queued for #4471
closes only the first.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Fixing the parser leaves the one-day moves open. Each problem now has its own
ticket.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

The counts are built from the ticket export at render time: duplicate rows from overlapping
export pages are counted once, and the reports the desk read as a one-day move
(`desk-notes.json`) are taken out of the swaps (`#lib/kb/problems`).

## The problems

### `swap`: day and month swap places

Every tenant on file writes the day first. The shared parser reads every date month first, so a
date whose day is 12 or lower comes out with day and month swapped: "Our 7 April reminder went
out on 4 July" (R-41200). This is the fix #4471 has queued. It covers every tenant and every
channel.

The table on #4471 splits these into `parser` and `hint`. The `hint` rule takes a report that
came in through the support form at a booking-v3 tenant as a booking made through the booking-v3
form. A report's channel is how it reached the desk, not how the booking was made, and the
reports in `hint` read exactly like the rest: the same swap, the same tenants' complaints. Nothing
in them points at a second bug, so they stay in this one ticket. If booking-v3's `MM/DD/YYYY`
hint turns out to cause swaps of its own, the parser fix will show it: those reports will keep
coming.

### `one-day`: the date moves by one day

These reports have no swap: "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11 May" (R-41202),
"Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting" (R-41344), "Every
booking in June is one day off" (R-41209). A day that moves by one looks like a time-zone or
daylight-saving problem in how invites are sent, not a parsing one. The desk had read these
apart (`oneDayMove`), but the #4471 table still counts them under `parser`, so a parser fix
would have been expected to close them.

## Intake tags are not the cause

Intake tags each report from the reporter's browser. The browser does not decide what went
wrong; the report does. Do not triage these problems by tag.

## The table on #4471, for comparison

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## What changes

- Each problem is tracked in its own ticket (linked in the table), with its own count. The fix
  queued for #4471 belongs to `swap`; `one-day` needs a fix of its own.
- New reports about a wrong date: read the body. A date one day off is `one-day`; a swapped day
  and month is `swap`.
- [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) accepted that reports with different
  subjects may end up apart. #4471 shows the other side: reports with the same subjects and
  different bugs end up together.

